Round-4 'acceleration' signal was |Delta-tau| ~ 0.02-0.35 (2-20% of tau_0).
If the spread above is comparable or larger, that signal is trajectory
noise -- and single-trajectory exponents in rounds 3-4 are noise-dominated.

================================================================================
VERDICT (2026-08-15), against the decision rule fixed before looking
================================================================================
Fixed-D spread is 72-105% of the mean (CV 23-49%). The round-4 "acceleration"
signal was 2-20% of tau_0. The signal is swamped by a factor of ~5-40x.

WORSE, and decisive for CLAIM-013: at D=0.05, THREE OF SIX realisations were
CENSORED (tau = nan) while three attained (tau = 1.008, 1.044, 2.663).
Attainment at that D is close to a coin flip. CLAIM-013's "perfectly ordered
censoring, no inversion" rested on exactly ONE sample per configuration; with
six samples at a single D the ordering already breaks.

Ensemble size required for a 5% measurement of the mean:
    D=0.0   CV=37%  -> n ~ 54
    D=0.018 CV=32%  -> n ~ 40
    D=0.05  CV=49%  -> n ~ 97
    D=0.1   CV=23%  -> n ~ 22

WHY THE BATTERY NEVER CAUGHT THIS. All six criteria test DETERMINISTIC
reproducibility -- same trajectory, finer discretisation (dt-refinement,
sampling adequacy) or a neighbouring parameter on the same trajectory family
(monotonicity, own-parameter stability). NONE tests STATISTICAL
reproducibility: a different trajectory drawn from the same physical
ensemble. In a chaotic system that is the binding constraint, and it was
absent from the battery by construction.

This also offers a UNIFIED explanation for the observable failures. Rather
than seven observables failing for six distinct reasons, the
parameter-discontinuity, own-parameter drift, non-monotonicity and B3'
failures are all consistent with ONE cause: single-trajectory sampling of a
chaotic system. (The horizon-divergence failures are separate and remain
real -- those concern the k_N^2 E ceiling, not sampling.)
